# What `npm start` runs with. Sign-in is switched off for now: every page and
# every API call goes straight through, the sign-in screen bounces back to the
# map, and everyone may edit and publish. Nothing is removed -- drop
# AUTH_ENABLED to bring the gate back, and the screen then offers
# Bypass (dev). Anything already set in the shell wins over this file, so to try
# Microsoft sign-in locally, export AUTH_TENANT_ID, AUTH_CLIENT_ID and
# AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET first.
AUTH_ENABLED=false
NODE_ENV=development
AUTH_DEV_BYPASS=true
# The Postgres from docker-compose.yml: `docker compose up -d postgres`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://domainmap:domainmap@localhost:5432/domainmap
# Who may edit and publish once sign-in is on, by the email they sign in with.
# Empty, everyone who signs in may. The bypass is always an owner.
OWNER_EMAILS=
# The model behind the Assistant. Both, or the Assistant writes prompts to copy
# into a chat of your own. Anything chat-completions shaped, or Anthropic's
# Messages API -- the URL says which, or ASSISTANT_API_STYLE does (openai |
# anthropic). Keep a real key out of this file: export it in the shell instead,
# which wins over anything here. A local Ollama wants any key at all:
#   ASSISTANT_API_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1/chat/completions
#   ASSISTANT_API_KEY=ollama
#   ASSISTANT_MODEL=llama3.1
# The server's own secret. Sign-in is off above, so no session is sealed with it
# here -- but a key for the Assistant's model set in the app (avatar -> Assistant
# model) is, and a server with no secret refuses to keep one. Anything long will
# do on a laptop; a deployment has a real one, which this is not.
AUTH_SESSION_SECRET=local-development-only-not-a-secret
